 ## Background
-Discontinuous Displacement Mapping was introduced by C. D. Correa, D. Silver, and M. Chen in 2006 [S1, unofficial]. It extends previous work on displacement mapping and volume rendering by addressing the challenge of rendering discontinuous transformations (cuts, fractures) in volume graphics. The method leverages hardware-accelerated texture sampling and fragment programs to achieve interactive frame rates. It builds on the concept of proxy geometry and inverse displacement mapping [S1, unofficial].
